WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.201
Hi, I'm Sandra from HEARa.

2
00:00:03.201 --> 00:00:06.720
Welcome to Day 10 of our 10-day Lip reading Challenge, 

3
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:11.820
designed to help you develop
and practice your speech reading skills! 

4
00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:15.000
Hearing loss can have a 
profound impact on your life. 

5
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.215
It touches every interaction.

6
00:00:17.215 --> 00:00:18.407
It can affect your career,

7
00:00:18.407 --> 00:00:20.034
your relationships,

8
00:00:20.034 --> 00:00:21.660
and your happiness.

9
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.939
Join me to explore tips and tricks
on this YouTube channel 

10
00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:32.940
to bring confidence and clarity
to relationships in all areas of your life. 

11
00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:39.300
I have so enjoyed creating this challenge for you.
I hope you've learned a lot and had fun too. 

12
00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:46.140
Your surprise for today is a Quiz.
This will give you a really good idea 

13
00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:50.400
of how much you've learned.
Details are below, and the answers are provided. 

14
00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:54.180
Through the last nine days
of the challenge, 

15
00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:58.260
you've learned
the most visible sounds in English. 

16
00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:03.840
We learned about these most visible sounds
in homophene groups, 

17
00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:04.974
where we could.

18
00:01:04.974 --> 00:01:09.000
Homophene groups are groups of sounds that look alike
on the lips. 

19
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:15.780
So the homophene groups
that we learned about are P, B, and M; 

20
00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:22.439
F and V;
and SH, CH, and J. 

21
00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:29.880
We also learned about the W sound,
the TH sound, and all about vowels. 

22
00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:34.799
Just as sounds can look alike,
words can look alike too. 

23
00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:40.200
Words that look the same
on the lips are called homophenous words, 

24
00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:43.424
and that's what we're going to practice today.

25
00:01:43.633 --> 00:01:47.334
For example, take the word roam.

26
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.684
There are three words that look like roam.

27
00:01:51.684 --> 00:01:54.216
What are they?

28
00:01:54.420 --> 00:02:01.200
Substitute the M for P or a B,
and consider alternate spellings. 

29
00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:05.520
If you're not sure,
the answers are written below. 

30
00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:09.600
Pause the video now
and write down the four words: 

31
00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:13.260
roam plus the three homophenous words. 

32
00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:17.760
I will say sentences silently
with these four words. 

33
00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:21.960
I'll give you some photos
for some context, 

34
00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:27.180
but hold on to the images
lightly in your mind and stay flexible. 

35
00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:31.980
We get into trouble
when we hold on to things like terriers. 

36
00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:37.260
Are you ready?
I'm going to say each sentence twice. 

37
